#{[17592186415208 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{13\\pi }{12}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415208 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415208 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "32debd00-39de-5d6e-9e96-d38fb37c9e00" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415214 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415214 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415214 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "485effef-da1a-51e4-aa09-f8d8650ea3ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415249 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{7\\pi }{12}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415249 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415249 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d067cbee-9b2c-5c36-8dc8-c47522a4af35" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415264 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415264 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415264 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415208 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415264 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415264 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415264 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415264 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415264 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186415199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415264 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9ad03362-7885-5567-9b35-4c8299722802" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415274 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415264 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415274 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125750" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415274 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415274 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,i+2y\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x\\,j.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-1,1)$ to $(\\sqrt{3},2)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415274 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3c651ce1-2ea6-5480-b7a8-389903a354b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415274 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-1,1)$ and $B(\\sqrt{3},2).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf={y}^{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\sqrt{3},2)-f(-1,1) \\\\  & =(2)^{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\sqrt{3}-(1)^{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{19\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419017 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454421 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186419017 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454422 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186419017 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454423 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186419017 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454424 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186419017 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125796" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419017 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419017 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨-y,\\,x⟩$ and the points $A(0,1)$ and $B(0,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne 0,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ traversed from $A$ to $B$ in counterclockwise direction.\n\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the segment line $\\overset{―}{AB},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$\n\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is independent of path.\n\n- **IV.** $F$ is conservative." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186419017 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b47df0de-b5ee-5562-b36a-a659cbe48d85" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419017 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Parameterizing the curve, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{1}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t{|}_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\pi  \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Parameterizing the line segment, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{2}(t)=⟨0,\\,1-2t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨2t-1,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-2⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ have the same initial and terminal points, yet\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$\n\nwe conclude that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is path-dependent.\n\n- Statement IV is false. If a vector field is conservative then it is path-independent. Since $F$ is path-dependent, it is not conservative." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424090 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186424098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424090 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186424099 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424090 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186424100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424090 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186424101 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424090 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186424102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424090 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424090 #edb/ref 1045 "Path Independence of Line Integrals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424090 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186424098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424090 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424090 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424090 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "a2243f17-089c-5e52-a019-0035fd16e9e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424091 #edb/ref 